<section class="py-20 px-6 bg-stone-light">
  <div class="max-w-[900px] mx-auto reveal">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-display font-bold text-ink tracking-tight mb-8 text-center">
      What Is Ancient Chinese Architecture?
    </h2>

    <div class="prose prose-lg prose-stone max-w-none text-ink-muted space-y-5 text-base leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Ancient Chinese architecture is one of the world's oldest continuous building traditions, spanning over
        <strong class="text-ink">3,000 years</strong> from the Shang dynasty (1600 BCE) through the fall of
        the Qing in 1911. Unlike the stone monuments of Egypt or Greece, Chinese builders perfected
        <strong class="text-ink">timber-frame construction</strong> — a system of interlocking wooden columns,
        beams, and brackets that proved remarkably resilient against earthquakes while allowing for wide,
        graceful roofs with deep overhanging eaves.
      </p>

      <p>
        The stakes of that system can be measured in surviving buildings. The <strong class="text-ink">East Hall of Foguang Temple</strong> (857 CE) has stood for over 1,100 years with no nails in its frame; the
        <strong class="text-ink">Yingxian Pagoda</strong> (1056 CE) has survived more than 40 recorded earthquakes,
        including the 7.8-magnitude Tangshan quake of 1976 that leveled concrete buildings nearby. At the heart of
        this resilience is the <strong class="text-ink">dougong</strong> (斗拱) — an interlocking bracket system that
        distributes roof weight onto columns and converts seismic energy into harmless friction, the same principle
        behind modern base isolators <a href="https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1279/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">(UNESCO, 2004)</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Beyond engineering, Chinese architecture is a <strong class="text-ink">written language of power</strong>.
        The color of a roof tile, the number of beasts on a ridge, and the width of a hall were regulated by law:
        yellow-glazed roofs belonged to the emperor alone, the Hall of Supreme Harmony's ten ridge beasts were the
        legal maximum, and commoners were barred from the hip roof form under penalty of prosecution. Every courtyard,
        axis, and gateway encodes a hierarchy — from the Forbidden City's 980 buildings laid along a 960-meter axis
        to the humblest Beijing siheyuan <a href="https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/439/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" class="text-vermillion hover:text-vermillion-dark transition-colors">(UNESCO, 1987)</a>. This language shaped East Asia: the temple architecture of Japan, the
        palaces of Korea, and the pagodas of Vietnam all descend from Chinese prototypes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Wood is mortal, and few buildings older than the Tang dynasty (618–907) survive above ground. What does
        remain — the <strong class="text-ink">Forbidden City</strong>, the <strong class="text-ink">Temple of Heaven</strong>,
        the remote Tang-era <strong class="text-ink">Foguang Temple</strong>, and cave temples carved with a hundred
        thousand Buddhist images — is largely <strong class="text-ink">open to visitors</strong>. Whether you are
        planning a trip, researching for a project, or simply curious about the roof you saw in a photograph,
        everything on this site is built to help you explore, understand, and experience China's architectural heritage.
      </p>
    </div>

